1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,724
The deploy job, the automated run that ships
your code, says "Repository not found".

2
00:00:09,764 --> 00:00:14,738
Your CTO turns the laptop around: "I am
looking at the repository in my browser.

3
00:00:14,778 --> 00:00:16,430
Which of the two is wrong?"

4
00:00:16,770 --> 00:00:21,254
Neither is wrong. The job's credential,
its proof of identity, can't see the

5
00:00:21,294 --> 00:00:24,468
repository, and GitHub answers
"not found" on purpose.

6
00:00:24,508 --> 00:00:27,385
The browser is signed in
as a different identity.

7
00:00:27,725 --> 00:00:32,601
A ticket for this incident usually quotes
one line: "fatal: Could not read from

8
00:00:32,641 --> 00:00:36,025
remote repository." That line
never contains the cause.

9
00:00:36,065 --> 00:00:41,128
Today you learn to read the whole stack of
lines, to say which program wrote each,

10
00:00:41,168 --> 00:00:44,925
and to walk from the text to the
root cause without guessing.

11
00:00:44,965 --> 00:00:47,725
Keep the CTO's laptop
in mind. It comes back.

12
00:00:49,565 --> 00:00:53,381
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:53,421 --> 00:00:58,247
This video closes the authentication
module. Authentication establishes which

14
00:00:58,287 --> 00:01:03,430
account a connection belongs to. Authorization,
a separate step, decides what that

15
00:01:03,470 --> 00:01:08,297
account may do. Four videos gave you the
mechanisms: the three questions, the

16
00:01:08,337 --> 00:01:13,416
credential helper protocol, tokens, SSH
with its configuration and host keys, and

17
00:01:13,456 --> 00:01:18,599
identities for people and machines. Now we
use all of it in one direction: from an

18
00:01:18,639 --> 00:01:20,116
error message backwards.

19
00:01:20,456 --> 00:01:25,325
The method has one first step, attribution:
which program printed this line?

20
00:01:25,365 --> 00:01:30,556
Then two tables, the SSH failures and the
HTTPS failures, one for each transport,

21
00:01:30,596 --> 00:01:35,271
each way Git reaches GitHub. And a
decision tree that puts them in order.

22
00:01:35,611 --> 00:01:41,601
The replays reproduce the failures inside
the lab, against local stand-ins, with no

23
00:01:41,641 --> 00:01:46,324
network: labs/ch16/failure-anatomy.sh
and the replay of Lab 20.3.

24
00:01:46,364 --> 00:01:52,064
Your real setup stays outside. Then the
walkthrough of Lab 20.3, Part B, in the

25
00:01:52,104 --> 00:01:58,021
normal shell, which reproduces three failures
against your own practice repository

26
00:01:58,061 --> 00:02:00,637
without changing your working setup.

27
00:02:00,977 --> 00:02:07,164
The diagnostic commands in this video are
🟢 SAFE: git remote -v, ssh -G, ssh-add -l,

28
00:02:07,204 --> 00:02:13,317
ssh -T, gh auth status. One reminder from
the chapter's table: gh auth token and gh

29
00:02:13,357 --> 00:02:19,100
auth status --show-token are 🔴 DANGEROUS,
because they print a live token, the

30
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:24,437
string that stands for your account,
to the terminal and its scrollback.

31
00:02:24,477 --> 00:02:27,403
They are not part of any diagnosis here.

32
00:02:29,243 --> 00:02:30,655
After this video you can:

33
00:02:30,995 --> 00:02:35,757
Say for an error line whether Git,
SSH, the helper or GitHub wrote it.

34
00:02:36,097 --> 00:02:41,241
Tell Permission denied (publickey) from
Permission to OWNER/REPO denied to USER and

35
00:02:41,281 --> 00:02:43,615
say what has been proved in each case.

36
00:02:43,955 --> 00:02:49,276
Diagnose Repository not found, Host key
verification failed, and Could not read from

37
00:02:49,316 --> 00:02:50,424
remote repository.

38
00:02:50,764 --> 00:02:54,249
Diagnose a 403 where Git
never asks for a credential.

39
00:02:54,589 --> 00:02:57,812
Walk the decision tree from the
error text to the root cause.

40
00:02:59,652 --> 00:03:03,940
An authentication error on your screen
is a short stack of lines written by

41
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:07,691
different programs. Reading it
starts with attributing each line.

42
00:03:08,031 --> 00:03:12,823
Lines that begin with ssh: are written by
your ssh client, the program that opens

43
00:03:12,863 --> 00:03:17,358
the connection. So are Permission denied
(publickey), "Host key verification

44
00:03:17,398 --> 00:03:21,713
failed." and the warning that the
remote host identification has changed.

45
00:03:21,753 --> 00:03:26,725
They mean the SSH connection or authentication
failed before Git's protocol started.

46
00:03:27,065 --> 00:03:32,670
Lines that begin with remote: over HTTPS
are written by GitHub's server and passed

47
00:03:32,710 --> 00:03:37,075
through. Over SSH, so are lines
such as the SSH key audit error.

48
00:03:37,115 --> 00:03:42,857
The server knows something and tells you:
repository not found, permission denied to

49
00:03:42,897 --> 00:03:44,302
a named user, a rule.

50
00:03:44,642 --> 00:03:49,059
"fatal: Could not read from remote
repository." and the two lines after it are

51
00:03:49,099 --> 00:03:52,659
written by Git. They mean the
other side closed the connection.

52
00:03:52,699 --> 00:03:54,659
This line never contains the cause.

53
00:03:54,999 --> 00:03:59,175
"fatal: Authentication failed
for", "fatal: repository ...

54
00:03:59,215 --> 00:04:05,282
not found" and "The requested URL returned
error" with a number are written by Git's

55
00:04:05,322 --> 00:04:09,790
HTTPS code, for the HTTP statuses
401, 404, or another status.

56
00:04:10,130 --> 00:04:14,783
And "fatal: could not read Username for"
is written by Git: no helper answered and

57
00:04:14,823 --> 00:04:19,305
prompting was impossible. A helper is the
program that stores your username and

58
00:04:19,345 --> 00:04:19,649
token.

59
00:04:19,989 --> 00:04:25,619
Now the SSH failures. Quick quiz: Permission
denied (publickey): has the server, A,

60
00:04:25,659 --> 00:04:30,402
refused your account this repository,
or B, matched no account at all?

61
00:04:30,442 --> 00:04:31,222
Your answer?

62
00:04:36,062 --> 00:04:39,403
B. Permission denied (publickey):
the server rejected the connection.

63
00:04:39,743 --> 00:04:44,151
None of the keys offered, the key pairs
that prove who you are, belongs to an

64
00:04:44,191 --> 00:04:47,329
account, or no key was offered,
or the user is not git.

65
00:04:47,369 --> 00:04:50,448
Almost everyone picks A at
first, so say it precisely.

66
00:04:50,488 --> 00:04:53,741
It does not mean "you lack
permission on the repository".

67
00:04:53,781 --> 00:04:58,594
The server hasn't looked at the repository
yet. It means the server could not attach

68
00:04:58,634 --> 00:05:02,869
your connection to any account. Nothing
has been proved about who you are.

69
00:05:03,209 --> 00:05:08,399
Permission to OWNER/REPO denied to USER:
the server again, and now something has

70
00:05:08,439 --> 00:05:13,695
been proved. The key authenticated as an
account that has no access here, or as a

71
00:05:13,735 --> 00:05:18,663
deploy key of another repository. A
deploy key is an SSH key attached to one

72
00:05:18,703 --> 00:05:22,520
repository, not to a person.
The message names who you are.

73
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:25,593
Authentication succeeded.
Authorization failed.

74
00:05:25,933 --> 00:05:31,919
"Repository not found" over SSH is the
same deliberate answer as over HTTPS.

75
00:05:31,959 --> 00:05:35,091
ssh -T git@github.com names the account.

76
00:05:35,431 --> 00:05:38,641
"Host key verification failed."
comes from your client.

77
00:05:38,681 --> 00:05:43,604
The server's host key, its proof of who it
is, differs from the one ssh remembers in

78
00:05:43,644 --> 00:05:47,149
known_hosts. If no announcement
explains it, do not connect.

79
00:05:47,489 --> 00:05:53,654
A connect error on port 22, refused or
timed out, is your client reporting the

80
00:05:53,694 --> 00:05:55,404
network. Try port 443.

81
00:05:55,744 --> 00:05:57,648
Now the HTTPS failures.

82
00:05:57,988 --> 00:06:03,364
"Repository not found" in a remote: line,
then Git's "not found": the server answers

83
00:06:03,404 --> 00:06:08,651
404 when the repository doesn't exist, or
when the credential has no access to it.

84
00:06:08,991 --> 00:06:14,529
"Authentication failed" is a 401. The
credential was rejected outright: a revoked or

85
00:06:14,569 --> 00:06:19,377
expired token, a password, a typo. Git
then tells the helper to erase it.

86
00:06:19,717 --> 00:06:25,030
"The requested URL returned error: 403":
the server knows who you are and refuses.

87
00:06:25,070 --> 00:06:30,188
No write permission, a classic token
without the needed scope or without single

88
00:06:30,228 --> 00:06:34,301
sign-on authorization, an
organization policy, or a rate limit.

89
00:06:34,341 --> 00:06:39,785
Git does not erase the credential. So this
is the 403 where Git never asks: a stored

90
00:06:39,825 --> 00:06:43,049
credential for another
account answers every time.

91
00:06:43,389 --> 00:06:48,531
"Could not read Username ... terminal
prompts disabled": no helper answered, and

92
00:06:48,571 --> 00:06:51,511
there is no terminal:
CI, cron, the lab shell.

93
00:06:51,851 --> 00:06:55,823
And "uses plaintext credentials":
the setting from the tokens video.

94
00:06:56,163 --> 00:07:01,956
One caveat the textbook marks unverified:
no GitHub documentation page explains HTTP

95
00:07:01,996 --> 00:07:07,649
403 on git push specifically. The list of
causes combines the cloning-errors page,

96
00:07:07,689 --> 00:07:13,204
the token documentation and the REST guide.
And the statement that Git keeps the

97
00:07:13,244 --> 00:07:18,967
credential after a 403 is from Git's source,
and was not observed against GitHub in

98
00:07:19,007 --> 00:07:19,730
the course.

99
00:07:20,070 --> 00:07:23,872
Why does GitHub answer "not
found" for something that exists?

100
00:07:23,912 --> 00:07:29,036
In the words of its REST troubleshooting
guide: "To avoid confirming the existence

101
00:07:29,076 --> 00:07:33,885
of private repositories". A 403 would
tell a stranger that the name is taken.

102
00:07:35,725 --> 00:07:40,010
A picture helps. Think of a relay of
three messengers between you and the

103
00:07:40,050 --> 00:07:43,328
repository. Your client program,
ssh or Git's HTTP code.

104
00:07:43,368 --> 00:07:46,349
The server's front door,
which decides who you are.

105
00:07:46,389 --> 00:07:50,141
And the server's repository rules,
which decide what you may do.

106
00:07:50,181 --> 00:07:54,762
Each messenger can come back with a
refusal, and each speaks in its own voice.

107
00:07:55,102 --> 00:07:59,561
The last line on your screen is usually
Git reporting that a messenger came back

108
00:07:59,601 --> 00:08:01,923
empty-handed. That's why you read upwards.

109
00:08:02,263 --> 00:08:07,305
The model breaks in one place, on purpose:
the front door and the repository rules

110
00:08:07,345 --> 00:08:12,200
sometimes give the same answer, "not
found", so that a stranger learns nothing.

111
00:08:12,240 --> 00:08:16,786
When you see that answer, you don't
try to guess which of the two refused.

112
00:08:16,826 --> 00:08:21,806
You ask the front door directly who it
thinks you are: ssh -T, or gh auth status.

113
00:08:23,646 --> 00:08:27,372
The root is the first of the
three questions: which transport?

114
00:08:27,412 --> 00:08:29,619
git remote get-url origin answers it.

115
00:08:29,959 --> 00:08:35,081
Try it now, thirty seconds: run
it in any repository of yours.

116
00:08:35,121 --> 00:08:37,079
SSH or HTTPS? I'll wait.

117
00:08:41,919 --> 00:08:46,288
Starting with git and an
at sign, or with ssh: SSH.

118
00:08:46,328 --> 00:08:48,623
Starting with https: HTTPS.

119
00:08:48,963 --> 00:08:53,470
The SSH branch goes outside in.
Network first: is there an ssh: line?

120
00:08:53,510 --> 00:08:58,677
Then the server's identity: the host key.
Then your identity: Permission denied

121
00:08:58,717 --> 00:09:03,027
(publickey) means no account matched,
and three commands show why.

122
00:09:03,067 --> 00:09:08,366
Then authorization: a named user, or "not
found", means you were authenticated as

123
00:09:08,406 --> 00:09:10,607
someone. ssh -T tells you as whom.

124
00:09:10,947 --> 00:09:15,325
The HTTPS branch has the same
order. No credential at all.

125
00:09:15,365 --> 00:09:21,495
A rejected credential, 401. Then 404 or
403: authenticated, or anonymous, and not

126
00:09:21,535 --> 00:09:26,370
allowed. Two commands: which helper
answered, and which account.

127
00:09:26,710 --> 00:09:32,289
And the last line, for both: does the same
identity work in the browser, or with gh

128
00:09:32,329 --> 00:09:36,689
repo view? That's the CTO's laptop
from the hook, used correctly.

129
00:09:41,029 --> 00:09:46,344
Exit status 2: no agent can be reached. The
agent is a background process that holds

130
00:09:46,384 --> 00:09:51,381
your decrypted keys. The replays run
without one. On your machine you meet this

131
00:09:51,421 --> 00:09:53,804
under sudo, in cron and in containers.

132
00:09:54,144 --> 00:09:59,086
Nothing listens on port 1 of this machine,
so the next failure is a real ssh error

133
00:09:59,126 --> 00:10:00,301
without any network.

134
00:10:02,641 --> 00:10:06,048
The same failing connection,
once alone and once under Git.

135
00:10:06,088 --> 00:10:09,552
Which lines will the second
output add, and who writes them?

136
00:10:09,892 --> 00:10:14,697
Alone, ssh reports the cause in one line.
Under Git, the same line is followed by

137
00:10:14,737 --> 00:10:18,645
Git's three-line trailer. The cause
is the line above the trailer.

138
00:10:18,985 --> 00:10:22,627
A stand-in for ssh that prints
its arguments and exits.

139
00:10:22,667 --> 00:10:23,765
The same trailer.

140
00:10:26,105 --> 00:10:29,866
This stand-in exits with
status 255 and prints nothing.

141
00:10:29,906 --> 00:10:31,732
What is left on the screen?

142
00:10:32,072 --> 00:10:35,814
Only the trailer. When ssh is
silent, there is no line above.

143
00:10:36,154 --> 00:10:41,054
A local path that does not exist: Git's
own check writes the cause, and then the

144
00:10:41,094 --> 00:10:41,857
same trailer.

145
00:10:42,197 --> 00:10:46,099
And a configured ssh program that
does not exist: "cannot exec".

146
00:10:46,139 --> 00:10:49,240
No trailer this time: Git
could not start anything.

147
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:05,639
A wrong user in the URL. The stand-in shows
that Git hands lab-user@github.com to

148
00:11:05,679 --> 00:11:11,172
ssh, and ssh -G confirms the user. On GitHub
that is Permission denied (publickey),

149
00:11:11,212 --> 00:11:14,105
because all connections
must be made as git.

150
00:11:14,445 --> 00:11:19,056
A command line that offers no key
at all: no agent, no identity file.

151
00:11:19,096 --> 00:11:24,178
-G shows it without connecting. This is
the form Part B uses against GitHub.

152
00:11:32,718 --> 00:11:34,877
Failure 1: no key is offered.

153
00:11:35,217 --> 00:11:39,391
Before you run it, predict both
outputs and what they prove about you.

154
00:11:39,431 --> 00:11:41,016
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

155
00:11:45,856 --> 00:11:49,159
The first is ssh alone. The
second adds Git's trailer.

156
00:11:49,199 --> 00:11:51,388
Proved about your identity: nothing.

157
00:11:51,728 --> 00:11:55,512
Failure 2: a stale host key,
in a scratch known_hosts file.

158
00:11:55,552 --> 00:11:57,586
Run this from the course folder.

159
00:11:57,926 --> 00:12:02,177
Your real known_hosts is not touched:
the command names a scratch file.

160
00:12:02,217 --> 00:12:06,408
Predict who writes the message: your
client. That's the check working.

161
00:12:06,748 --> 00:12:11,816
Failure 3: a wrong credential for a repository
that does not exist, then the right

162
00:12:11,856 --> 00:12:12,501
credential.

163
00:12:12,841 --> 00:12:17,477
The first command uses the one-command
reset from video 118, so your stored

164
00:12:17,517 --> 00:12:22,341
credential is neither read nor erased.
Predict the status for each: a rejected

165
00:12:22,381 --> 00:12:26,518
credential, then a valid credential
for a name that does not exist.

166
00:12:26,558 --> 00:12:31,381
Compare the two messages and decide which
of them tells you anything about the

167
00:12:31,421 --> 00:12:32,067
repository.

168
00:12:33,907 --> 00:12:35,555
Five mistakes to watch for.

169
00:12:35,895 --> 00:12:39,503
Quoting only "Could not read from
remote repository" in a ticket.

170
00:12:39,543 --> 00:12:44,104
Root cause: Git writes that line when the
other program exits; the cause is in the

171
00:12:44,144 --> 00:12:44,889
line above it.

172
00:12:45,229 --> 00:12:49,032
Reading Permission denied (publickey)
as missing repository access.

173
00:12:49,072 --> 00:12:53,677
Root cause: the server could not attach
the connection to any account; it has not

174
00:12:53,717 --> 00:12:55,110
looked at the repository.

175
00:12:55,450 --> 00:12:59,134
Concluding from "Repository not
found" that the URL is wrong.

176
00:12:59,174 --> 00:13:04,139
Root cause: the server gives the same answer
when the credential has no access, to

177
00:13:04,179 --> 00:13:07,130
avoid confirming that
private repositories exist.

178
00:13:07,470 --> 00:13:12,806
Re-entering credentials after a 403.
Root cause: Git does not erase a stored

179
00:13:12,846 --> 00:13:18,182
credential on 403, so the same wrong
account answers again without a prompt.

180
00:13:18,522 --> 00:13:22,801
Changing SSH settings to fix an
HTTPS remote, or the reverse.

181
00:13:22,841 --> 00:13:27,119
Root cause: the first question,
which transport, was skipped.

182
00:13:28,959 --> 00:13:34,208
Now, out of the lab. The deploy job
from the hook, walked down the tree.

183
00:13:34,248 --> 00:13:39,790
Step 1: git remote get-url origin in
the job's checkout prints an HTTPS URL.

184
00:13:39,830 --> 00:13:45,887
HTTPS branch. The full error has a remote:
line, "Repository not found", then Git's

185
00:13:45,927 --> 00:13:51,029
"not found": a 404. Step 4h: which
helper answered, and which account?

186
00:13:51,369 --> 00:13:56,112
The trace shows the helper. The job's token
turns out to be a fine-grained token,

187
00:13:56,152 --> 00:14:00,482
the kind that can be limited to selected
repositories, created for another

188
00:14:00,522 --> 00:14:05,029
repository of the same organization. The
repository in question is not in its

189
00:14:05,069 --> 00:14:09,930
selection. The server authenticated the
token and answered as if the repository did

190
00:14:09,970 --> 00:14:10,520
not exist.

191
00:14:10,860 --> 00:14:14,108
The CTO's browser was right
too: his account can see it.

192
00:14:14,148 --> 00:14:18,628
The last line of the tree would have
caught the difference at once: the job's

193
00:14:18,668 --> 00:14:21,857
identity does not work with
gh repo view, and his does.

194
00:14:22,197 --> 00:14:27,123
The fix is in the token's repository
selection, or better, an app installation that

195
00:14:27,163 --> 00:14:32,030
includes the repository. The team's prevention
is the one in the box: one identity

196
00:14:32,070 --> 00:14:35,620
per host or alias, chosen by
configuration and not by habit.

197
00:14:35,660 --> 00:14:38,372
And a rule for tickets:
quote the whole error.

198
00:14:40,212 --> 00:14:43,889
Your turn. Do Lab 20.3, "Three
failures, reproduced and diagnosed", in

199
00:14:43,929 --> 00:14:48,349
lab-manual/m20-authentication-ssh.md. For
each failure, before you produce it, write

200
00:14:48,389 --> 00:14:52,650
down three things: the exact error text
you expect, which program will write each

201
00:14:52,690 --> 00:14:56,526
line, and what will have been proved
about your identity when you see it.

202
00:14:56,866 --> 00:15:03,146
The challenge is Exercise 20.6,
"Three faults, no connection", in

203
00:15:03,186 --> 00:15:05,869
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

204
00:15:07,709 --> 00:15:10,850
Question 232 of the CTO question bank:

205
00:15:13,190 --> 00:15:17,250
Answer out loud. A strong answer says
for each message which check failed,

206
00:15:17,290 --> 00:15:21,738
authentication or authorization, and
therefore what the server knows about you at

207
00:15:21,778 --> 00:15:26,226
that moment. It names the commands that
produce the evidence in each case and the

208
00:15:26,266 --> 00:15:30,658
different fixes that follow. It does not
use the word "permission" for the first

209
00:15:30,698 --> 00:15:32,320
message without correcting it.

210
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:35,087
Let's land this.

211
00:15:35,427 --> 00:15:37,087
You should now be able to say:

212
00:15:37,427 --> 00:15:42,690
Attribute every line first: ssh: lines are
the client, remote: lines are the server,

213
00:15:42,730 --> 00:15:45,405
and Git's trailer never
contains the cause.

214
00:15:45,745 --> 00:15:50,079
Permission denied (publickey) means no
account matched; a message naming a user

215
00:15:50,119 --> 00:15:52,737
means you were authenticated
and not authorized.

